ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Erectile Dysfunction (Psychogenic) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Organic Vascular Erectile Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of rigid nocturnal/morning erections and the sudden, context-specific onset, which confirms the vascular supply is completely intact.

Neurological Deficit - Ruled out/unlikely due to normal perineal sensation, intact reflexes, and no history of spinal trauma.

Medication-Induced Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient is not taking any pharmacological agents.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of acute panic driven by sudden-onset psychogenic erectile dysfunction. The preservation of nocturnal and masturbatory erections definitively confirms that the physiological, vascular, and neurological mechanisms required for an erection are perfectly intact. The dysfunction is entirely driven by sympathetic nervous system overdrive (performance anxiety) directly inhibiting parasympathetic erectile pathways. An urgent after-hours visit was essential to perform a focused physical examination, definitively rule out an acute organic pathology, and rapidly de-escalate the severe acute anxiety state. Deferring this assessment would have allowed the panic and relationship crisis to escalate unnecessarily.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical examination to definitively demonstrate intact neurological and vascular function to the patient. Provided extensive reassurance and psychoeducation explaining the physiological "fight or flight" response blocking the erection. Prescribed a short, very low-dose course of oral Diazepam (2mg PRN) strictly to break the acute panic cycle tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: To dismantle performance anxiety, the patient is instructed to practice 'sensate focus'—engaging in physical intimacy with their partner with a strict, agreed-upon ban on any attempt at intercourse. By removing the pressure to 'perform', the sympathetic nervous system relaxes. Experiencing intimacy without the demand for an erection will positively reinforce a state of relaxation, naturally allowing the parasympathetic nervous system to function again.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral generated for a specialized sex therapist or psychologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to check mental health status and coordinate the psychology referral.

